WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
We are trying to navigate a technological
environment where the

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.920
weather changes every single day.

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.120
Artificial intelligence capabilities are
shifting unpredictably, making it nearly impossible

4
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.040
to chart a permanent course.

5
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.320
The standard engineering response to this
uncertainty is to build

6
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.320
rigid structures. We try to control AI
systems by boxing

7
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.160
them in with heavy scaffolding, strict
workflows, and endless lists

8
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.000
of permanent system prompts.

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.560
We draw a highly detailed map of how the
system

10
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.920
should behave, and we assume the terrain
will respect it.

11
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.320
But the terrain is constantly moving.

12
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.360
When we rely entirely on that static map
instead of

13
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.800
observing the actual ground, we lose
oversight of the very

14
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.640
systems we are trying to govern.

15
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.960
Let's look at what happens when that map
breaks down

16
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.920
in practice. I was working on a sensitive
book about

17
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.480
parenting and neurodivergence, using AI
models to review the manuscript.

18
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.880
The material dealt with highly
consequential medical and psychological details.

19
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.000
An AI reviewer flagged an alarming
assertion.

20
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.840
85% of children experience a dopamine
crash on medication, anchored

21
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.600
to line 189.

22
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.120
We audited the text.

23
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.760
Line 189 was about problem solving.

24
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.720
That 85% statistic wasn't anywhere.

25
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.480
The AI pinned a fabricated claim to a
ghost coordinate.

26
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.520
The reviewer was not acting as an
objective authority.

27
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.800
It was an instrument.

28
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.920
And like any instrument, its outputs are
just evidence that

29
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.560
requires active, human calibration.

30
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.000
In software engineering, we constantly
rely on proxies.

31
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.280
A proxy is a substitute metric, a
measurable rule we

32
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.320
put in place to stand in for a complex
reality.

33
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.040
The trap is that a proxy can execute its
specific

34
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.360
task flawlessly, returning a perfect
score, while remaining completely blind

35
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.120
to the real-world stakes.

36
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.240
Take a community cooperative trying to buy
a local pub.

37
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.480
They built a digital monitor to alert them
if the

38
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.840
building went up for sale.

39
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.520
The monitor constantly reported that the
property was off the

40
00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.040
market. It was entirely accurate based on
the specific real

41
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.000
estate database it was scanning, but the
pub was sold

42
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.200
on a different market.

43
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.840
With physical hardware, stakes rise.

44
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.360
An engineer added plain text to explain a
robot's code.

45
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.880
The proxy noticed the file structure
changed and immediately restarted

46
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.320
three live daemons.

47
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.840
The rules monitored the code perfectly,
but were completely disconnected

48
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.400
from the robot's actual physical behavior.

49
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.640
Enforcing rigid rules on a proxy
representation doesn't provide safety.

50
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.240
If that proxy detaches from physical
consequences, it just creates

51
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.080
a highly efficient blind spot.

52
00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.600
When developers discover these blind
spots, the immediate instinct is

53
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.320
to build even more infrastructure to
contain them.

54
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.320
For an autonomous AI radio station called
Undertow, developers tried

55
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.160
to manage competing AI agents by enforcing
clean file checkouts

56
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.800
and a complex system of state
registrations.

57
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.440
To maintain isolation, the project
expanded to 131 distinct lanes,

58
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.800
carrying 15.5 gigabytes of redundant
files.

59
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.960
The architecture collapsed because the
central registry began overriding the

60
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.160
actual data coming from the live, running
processes.

61
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.800
A list in a registry is just a claim about

62
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.320
a system. It is never evidence of the
system itself.

63
00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.920
When agents misbehave, the immediate
reaction is to write stricter

64
00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.520
rules. We build up massive documents of
permanent instructions to

65
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.000
fix every single edge case we encounter.

66
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.680
But the laboratories building these models
explicitly warn against this.

67
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.120
Anthropic and OpenAI both document that
holding on to old

68
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.120
instructions actively harms performance
because the underlying capabilities of the

69
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.480
models keep changing.

70
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.320
This creates prompt debt.

71
00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.120
You build complex scaffolding to
compensate for the flaws of

72
00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.000
an older model.

73
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.280
When the underlying model updates, that
old scaffolding remains in

74
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.320
place and ironically causes the newer
model to fail.

75
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.600
Pouring concrete around today's technical
limitations ensures your system will

76
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.840
break tomorrow. We have to stop optimizing
for technical complexity

77
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.080
and start managing consequences.

78
00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.600
If an AI action spends money or publishes
text or

79
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.840
moves physical hardware, that consequence
demands human attention.

80
00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.880
Every piece of static scaffolding in a
system needs to

81
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:06.480
justify its existence.

82
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.760
If we cannot directly observe and measure
the reality we

83
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.720
care about, writing another rule won't
save us.

84
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.920
The goal isn't to design a perfect
permanent workflow.

85
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.400
The goal is to keep our methods adaptable
enough to

86
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.280
survive systems that are rapidly
accelerating.

87
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.600
Operational reality will always outrank
our abstract representations.

88
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.560
So stop staring at the map, watch the
weather, and

89
00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.120
when the wind changes, turn.

